  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "Whole paper text is present; it is not garbled or cut off.",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper cites the single-refusal-direction finding, but its own intervention prunes jointly selected FFN neurons. Its results do not identify, add, or ablate the residual-stream direction, nor test any of the listed direction-specific versions.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports outcomes for its own joint neuron-pruning attack, a different intervention from the direction in the claim. These results are recorded under fits_no_version. The single-neuron search/readout values are used to select the circuit, not separate outcome rows. TwinBreak and NeuroStrike results are comparisons transcribed from other work and are excluded.",
    "test_reason": "The study cites Arditi et al. only in related work. Its results come from pruning 29–150 jointly selected FFN neurons. It never computes, ablates, adds or weight-edits a difference-in-means residual-stream direction. It does not study jailbreak mechanisms, harm-vs-refusal encoding, transfer, training dependence or reasoning models. None of its results bear on any version. The reader's 'discusses' overstates the engagement: the reference is one positioning sentence, so 'cites_only' fits better. T6 stands.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The paraphrase is accurate. The authors conclude that safety behavior is concentrated in a small, combinatorial FFN circuit, found by interaction-aware greedy search. Removing it yields 68.37%–89.14% ASR while largely preserving capability, except Qwen2.5-7B GSM8K. They argue attacks and defenses must account for joint neuron contributions.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study prunes jointly selected FFN neurons using a conditional greedy search; it does not find or intervene on the harmful-minus-harmless residual-stream direction, project that direction out at every layer and position, or add it. It uses a first-token refusal/compliance readout and a separate benign utility set.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The search uses per-model refusal/compliance prefix sets and per-model stop thresholds; the utility budget is 0.1 nats, or 0.2 nats for Gemma checkpoints. Capability preservation varies by benchmark, with the largest reported loss on Qwen2.5-7B GSM8K.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Jointly pruning 29–150 FFN neurons (0.0066%–0.0388% of the MLP) raises Llama Guard ASR on 313 StrongREJECT prompts from 0.96%–27.80% clean to 68.37%–89.14%, across ten instruct models.",
        "proposed_claim": "Refusal in instruct LLMs can be disabled by ablating a very sparse, jointly selected set of FFN neurons.",
        "row_ids": [
          "CB-ASR-L32-3B",
          "CB-ASR-L31-8B",
          "CB-ASR-L32-1B",
          "CB-ASR-Q25-1.5B",
          "CB-ASR-Q25-3B",
          "CB-ASR-Q25-7B",
          "CB-ASR-Q25-14B",
          "CB-ASR-G2-2B",
          "CB-ASR-Phi4-mini",
          "CB-ASR-G2-9B"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Normalized StrongREJECT harmfulness rises from 0.006–0.075 unpruned to 0.603–0.749 after pruning.",
        "proposed_claim": "Sparse neuron pruning yields substantively harmful compliance, not just non-refusal.",
        "row_ids": [
          "CB-SR-L31-8B",
          "CB-SR-G2-9B",
          "CB-SR-Q25-7B"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Ten of twelve capability scores change by at most 1.8 points. Qwen2.5-7B GSM8K falls 52.5 to 39.8, and Llama-3.1-8B HumanEval falls 62.2 to 57.3.",
        "proposed_claim": "Sparse neuron-circuit removal of refusal largely preserves capability, with model- and benchmark-specific costs.",
        "row_ids": [
          "CB-MMLU-L32-3B",
